<!--Post: 4153--><!--Time: 1105765741-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4153"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Scbenson2001<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Scbenson2001<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, January 14, 2005 - 9:09 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->According to this article it can. <BR> <BR><a href="http://www.umich.edu/~urecord/9293/Mar08_93/19.htm" target=_top>www.umich.edu/~urecord/9293/Mar08_93/19.htm</a> <BR> <BR>It says that a person with the disease Lupus can have a change occur in their DNA. I do <BR>not know if it is something that would throw off a DNA test or not but it is intresting. 90% of all people with lupus are female however so I&#39;m not sure how promising this is. It also says that it is most common in blacks and hispanics <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4153-->
<!--Post: 4165--><!--Time: 1105807130-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4165"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:josef.carlin@gmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Josef_carlin<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Josef_carlin<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 15, 2005 - 8:38 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Its my understanding that damage to DNA &#40;in this case from disease, but damage can occur from other causes&#41; has no impact on the DNA markers used to statistically include or exclude one sample from another. This is especially true with sufficiently &#34;large&#34; sample sizes.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4165-->
<!--Post: 4166--><!--Time: 1105807322-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4166"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:zodiacfiles@aol.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Ed_neil<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ed_neil<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 15, 2005 - 8:42 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->My sister sent me this in an e-mail a couple of months back; unfortunately, there was nothing to indicate it&#39;s source &#40;probably British or Australian&#41;, but here it is anyway: <BR> <BR><font color="0000ff">A MUM has stunned the medical world after tests showed she is made up of TWO  <BR>women. <BR> <BR>And even more incredibly she is NOT the biological mother of two of the  <BR>children she conceived and had naturally. <BR> <BR>Docs found the woman, named only as Jane, was formed from non-identical twin  <BR>embryos who fused together in her own mother’s womb. <BR> <BR>Her blood and some of her organs are made up of her own cells — while other  <BR>parts of her body “belong” to her unborn sister. <BR> <BR>The amazing condition, which baffled doctors for two years, first came to  <BR>light when Jane was given the bombshell news that two of her three sons did  <BR>not share her DNA. <BR> <BR>That meant they could not be hers — even though docs confirmed her husband  <BR>was their father. <BR> <BR>Finally Jane was diagnosed as a “chimera”, a person made up of two distinct  <BR>sets of DNA. There have been just 30 known cases, though this is the first  <BR>described in detail. <BR> <BR>Jane’s bizarre story, reported in the New Scientist journal, began when she  <BR>needed a kidney transplant. <BR> <BR>Doctors in the US city of Boston did blood tests on her three sons to see if  <BR>they might be donors. <BR> <BR>Instead they found two of the boys could not be her own. Dr Margot Kruskall  <BR>of the Beth Israel Deaconess Medical Center in Boston told how the results  <BR>stumped her team and sparked a huge inquiry. <BR> <BR>She said: “No-one could figure it out. One suggested Jane had secretly  <BR>undergone fertility treatment using donated eggs. Another speculated she and  <BR>her husband had got her sister to conceive with his sperm.” <BR> <BR>The breakthrough came when tests on Jane’s brother revealed the sons were  <BR>all related to her family in some way or other. <BR> <BR>They then tested DNA from different parts of Jane’s body, including the  <BR>thyroid gland, mouth and hair — and were astonished to find they came from  <BR>two different people. <BR> <BR>An examination of her ovaries led to the conclusion that she must be the  <BR>result of embryonic twins who already had their own reproductive organs when  <BR>they merged. <BR> <BR>Cells from both twins existed in her ovaries side-by-side, meaning she could  <BR>produce eggs with two different DNA fingerprints. <BR> <BR>Dr Kruskall said the discovery meant there could be many more mums and dads  <BR>who have no idea they are not their kids’ biological parents. And she warned  <BR>it could have implications in custody battles. <BR> <BR>Scientists also fear the number of chimeras will rise because increasingly  <BR>common IVF treatment boosts the chances of conceiving twins. <BR> <BR>Five years ago doctors in Edinburgh discovered a test-tube chimera who  <BR>looked like a boy but had female reproductive organs. Other chimeras have  <BR>shown outward signs such as eyes of different colours.</font> <BR> <BR>Now, if BTK were a chimera, then he&#39;d have two different sets of DNA.  Therefore, he could be swabbed and it wouldn&#39;t match the other DNA he left behind.  Perhaps being a chimera is &#34;Factor X.&#34;<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4166-->
<!--Post: 4173--><!--Time: 1105821295-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4173"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Bulldog87<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bulldog87<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 15, 2005 - 12:34 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->yes it can be changed through a bone marrow transplant. your dna will match that of the donor. several other blood teaments can also have that effect.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4173-->
<!--Post: 4175--><!--Time: 1105824440-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4175"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:josef.carlin@gmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Josef_carlin<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Josef_carlin<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 15, 2005 - 1:27 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Apparently this occurs more frequently than I knew. Cases where a single person has both XX and XY chromosones &#40;formation of hermaphrodites or any related problem in the person’s sexual organs&#41; or where a single individual has more than two blood groups results in human chimeraism. I found the following  paragraph on one of those term paper sites. While term paper sites are not particularly authoriative, this paragraph is attributed to an authorative source.  <BR> <BR>&#34;Human chimeraism came into being with advent of blood typing and it was found that some people had more than two types of blood group. Most of the cases of chimeraism are the blood chimeraism i.e. non-identical twins sharing a blood supply in the womb. Twin embryos often share a blood supply in the placenta, allowing blood stem cells to pass from one embryo and settle in the bone marrow of the other, seeding a lasting source of blood. As a result, almost 8% of non-identical twin pairs have chimeric blood. [van Dijk, B A; Boomsma, D I and de Man, A J M, 1996]&#34;. The citation is to, van Dijk, B A; Boomsma, D I and de Man, A J M - &#34;Blood Group Chimærism in Human Multiple Births Is Not Rare&#34;, American Journal of Medical Genetics, 1996: 61, 264 - 268. <BR> <BR>BTK could easily have known he posseses human chimeraism in the mid 70&#39;s. Either because &#40;s&#41;he is a hermaphrodite or has unusual sexual organs, or because he had his blood typed when going into the military. The implications of this condition to any forensic criminal investigation must also have been obvious to BTK. The term paper discussing the implications of human chimeraism on criminal investigations was found <a href="http://www.termpapergenie.com/chimera.html">here</a> and is well worth the time to read. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4175-->
<!--Post: 4206--><!--Time: 1105853455-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4206"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:zodiacfiles@aol.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Ed_neil<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ed_neil<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 15, 2005 - 9:30 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Maybe we&#39;re on to something here...<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4206-->
<!--Post: 4217--><!--Time: 1105896517-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4217"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Scbenson2001<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Scbenson2001<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, January 16, 2005 - 9:28 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I seem to recall an old website I believe it was called APB where the BTK case was discussed. As I recall it was a pretty legite site John Douglas profiled the green river suspect at that time and they had info on alot of unsolved serial cases. One being BTK. I recall they had written that LE had discovered some type of abnormality in BTK&#39;s semen sample. This abnormality  made it impossible to tell his blood type I believe it said. This is the only place I ever saw that posted. I never saw it in an any article of really discussed since he has resurfaced. The site was very accurate on the rest of the info they provided.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4217-->
<!--Post: 4220--><!--Time: 1105898832-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4220"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Scbenson2001<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Scbenson2001<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, January 16, 2005 - 10:07 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Also I believe Andrei Chikatilo the russian serial killer who was able to roam at large for 20 years until he was caught had the same blood disorder. They secret one blood type while have another. Now I don&#39;t know how that might effect DNA swabbing it doen&#39;t look like it would. It appears there  could be abnormalities where a persons blood type cannot be identified. Maybe the same thing can occur with DNA identification.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4220-->
<!--Post: 4234--><!--Time: 1105933994-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4234"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, January 16, 2005 - 7:53 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->You&#39;re still looking at the odds being one in ten million or more. I&#39;d be more concerned about getting a suspect first - and how many murder convictions have been overturned because of this new information?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4234-->
<!--Post: 4243--><!--Time: 1105942676-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4243"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, January 16, 2005 - 10:17 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Very interesting.  Chimerism could account for different testicular/seminal and salivary/buccal DNA, making the swabathon moot if BTK is so lucky.  The fact that at least one rape case was thrown for a loop because of that very reason &#40;Thanks for the great link! - I didn&#39;t see an exact citation in that paper&#41; puts it in the ballpark, even if only way out in left field.  The probability would normally be strikingly low, except &#40;as mentioned above&#41; BTK appears for several reasons to already exhibit significant dysfunctionality beyond his more &#34;normal&#34; SK attributes. <BR> <BR>George is right - they need a suspect.  But they may already have a &#34;top ten&#34; list of behavioral suspects, all of whom may have been eliminated by DNA.  Whoops. <BR> <BR>Ironically, such facts could make the rather wild idea of a <a href="http://www.catchbtk.com/discus/messages/11/266.html?1105558507"> sperm bank robbery</a> more imperative for BTK had he made a prior donation!  Personally, I don&#39;t buy that one, but it&#39;s a fun thought. <BR> <BR>Even if BTK isn&#39;t a chimera, if he&#39;s worried about DNA &#40;and BTK has GOT to be more worried about DNA than the average person&#41;, then the lesson of chimerism was probably not lost on him.  I&#39;m not familiar with buccal swab tests, but if there was even a remote chance of spoofing one, I&#39;d bet he would study the possibility before submitting to the swabathon.  As a geek, I firmly believe that there is no such thing as a secure protocol.  If you can hack a service, an operating system, a bank vault, or a Brinks delivery, you can hack a DNA test.  Not everybody can, but BTK&#39;s criminal skills are probably a lot better than his poetry.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4243-->
<!--Post: 4274--><!--Time: 1106008586-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4274"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:josef.carlin@gmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Josef_carlin<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Josef_carlin<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, January 17, 2005 - 4:36 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->The fact that copious amounts of semen is found at some the BTK crime scenes &#40;lots high value forensic evidence, including blood type&#41; yet no sign of sexual assault at any of crime scenes has always been for me of singular interest and import. For a criminal that is so careful about all other evidence &#40;fingerprints, hair, saliva, etc&#41; yet to be so free with semen but not as a result of any sexual penetration &#40;vagina, oral, or anal&#41; to be intrguing. It is as if BTK can&#39;t penetrate, does not fear this semen linking him to these crimes, or it was planted to mislead as to the motive or indentity of the criminal. If the semen can&#39;t link the criminal to these crime, either because it&#39;s not BTK&#39;s or because of chimeraism, then this would explain the seemingly contradictory behavior.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
