<!--Post: 4279--><!--Time: 1106014937-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4279"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Weeza<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Weeza<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, January 17, 2005 - 6:22 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Well, and the condition of the semen itself can show how long it was there as it dies within a few hours in open air. &#40; it survives much longer inside say a  body cavity&#41;. <BR>I even answered a question earlier this week about the sheer amount of semen, and whether or not it could be stolen from a sperm bank...but they could tell if it was thawed. <BR>Well, I&#39;m learning facts I never wanted to know, but its still fascinating. <BR>My guess is that he has no priors of sexual assualt, at least not any worry that any samples could be tied to him through DNA. I only learned of the existance of Chimeras last year, so all of this is bizarrely new and fascinating to me. <BR>Its my understanding that those with the condition will have the same DNA is say hair and saliva, but  in blood or semen, it its completely different.Perhaps one set of DNA is saved in this investigation &#40; semen&#41; but the other set is unknown, which would explain his, um, generosity with bodily fluids. <BR>I think this whole theory is remote, but fascinating, but still possible. <BR> <BR><a href="http://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/main.jhtml?xml=/news/2003/11/13/nivf113.xml&amp;sSheet=/news/2003/11/13/ixhome.html" target=_top>http://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/main.jhtml?xml=/ne ws/2003/11/13/nivf113.xml&amp;sSheet=/news/2003/11/13/ ixhome.html</a><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4279-->
<!--Post: 4282--><!--Time: 1106023651-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4282"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, January 17, 2005 - 8:47 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Thanks for the additional link, Weeza.  One line is particularly telling:  &#34;Around 30 similar instances of chimerism have been reported, and there are probably many more who will never discover their unusual origins. Most chimeras probably go through life unaware of their unusual constitution.&#34; <BR> <BR>At the risk of being a bit too cute, it appears that we are now speculating about BTK having &#34;multiple person disorder&#34;.  Quite X-Files.  I found another link talking about discovery of chimeras during Olympic testing, but I can&#39;t make the link post successfully. <BR> <BR>As you say, rather remote, but when swab number 2,000,000 comes back negative, it may be time to look at the strange guy that good old shoe-leather detective work says is BTK.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
